Exit with status 2 when the command line names an unknown -o option.
An invalid option letter on the shell's command line, as in "sh -Z",
makes the shell exit with status 2, but an unknown option name, as in
"sh -o bogus", made it print the error and exit with status 0, because
procargs() raised the error without setting the exit status, so a
caller could not tell that the shell had not run its script. bash and
dash exit with status 2.

Set the exit status to 2 before raising the error. "set -o bogus"
inside the shell is unchanged: the set builtin still fails with
status 1 and the shell carries on.

---
 shell/ash.c |    1 +
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+)

--- a/shell/ash.c
+++ b/shell/ash.c
@@ -16778,6 +16778,7 @@
 		optlist[i] = 2;
 	if (options(&login_sh)) {
 		/* it already printed err message */
+		exitstatus = 2;
 		raise_exception(EXERROR); /* does not return */
 	}
 	xargv = argptr;
